{"acceptance_length": 1, "acceptance_lengths": [], "accepted_draft_lengths": [], "benchmark": "math500", "completion": "We are given the coordinates of three vertices of a **parallelogram**:  \n$$\n(5, 3),\\ (6, 8),\\ (7, 4)\n$$  \nand the fourth vertex is $(x, y)$, with the condition that $x > 7$. We are to find the value of $x + y$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the properties of a parallelogram\n\nIn a **parallelogram**, the **opposite sides are equal and parallel**, and the **diagonals bisect each other**.\n\nThis means that the **midpoint of the diagonals** is the same.\n\nSo, if we can find the diagonals, we can use the midpoint formula to find the missing point.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Label the points\n\nLet\u2019s label the given points for clarity:\n\n- $A = (5, 3)$\n- $B = (6, 8)$\n- $C = (7, 4)$\n- $D = (x, y)$ (unknown)\n\nWe need to determine which of these points are connected to form the parallelogram.\n\nThere are **three possible configurations** for the parallelogram, depending on which two points are adjacent and which are opposite.\n\nLet\u2019s try all possible configurations and see which one gives a consistent result.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Try configuration: A, B, C, D in order\n\nAssume the parallelogram is $ABCD$, with sides $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, and $DA$.\n\nThen the diagonals are $AC$ and $BD$.\n\nLet\u2019s compute the midpoint of diagonal $AC$:\n\n$$\n\\text{Midpoint of } AC = \\left( \\frac{5 + 7}{2}, \\frac{3 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left(6, \\frac{7}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nNow, the midpoint of diagonal $BD$ should be the same:\n\n$$\n\\text{Midpoint of } BD = \\left( \\frac{6 + x}{2}, \\frac{8 + y}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nSet this equal to the midpoint of $AC$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6 + x}{2} = 6 \\Rightarrow 6 + x = 12 \\Rightarrow x = 6\n$$\n$$\n\\frac{8 + y}{2} = \\frac{7}{2} \\Rightarrow 8 + y = 7 \\Rightarrow y = -1\n$$\n\nBut this gives $x = 6$, which **violates the condition** $x > 7$. So this configuration is **not valid**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Try another configuration\n\nTry the configuration: $A, B, D, C$\n\nSo the diagonals are $AD$ and $BC$\n\nCompute midpoint of $AD$:\n\n$$\n\\text{Midpoint of } AD = \\left( \\frac{5 + x}{2}, \\frac{3 + y}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nCompute midpoint of $BC$:\n\n$$\n\\text{Midpoint of } BC = \\left( \\frac{6 + 7}{2}, \\frac{8 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n$$\n\nSet the midpoints equal:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2} \\Rightarrow 5 + x = 13 \\Rightarrow x = 8\n$$\n$$\n\\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6 \\Rightarrow 3 + y = 12 \\Rightarrow y = 9\n$$\n\nNow we have $x = 8$, $y = 9$, and $x > 7$ is satisfied.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Final Answer\n\n$$\nx + y = 8 + 9 = \\boxed{17}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{17}\n$$", "draft_calls": 0, "input_ids": [151644, 872, 198, 785, 13934, 315, 264, 57030, 839, 2396, 525, 320, 20, 11, 220, 18, 701, 320, 21, 11, 220, 23, 701, 320, 22, 11, 220, 19, 8, 323, 4930, 87, 11, 379, 15087, 323, 400, 87, 861, 220, 22, 12947, 3555, 374, 279, 897, 315, 400, 87, 488, 379, 3, 5267, 5501, 2874, 3019, 553, 3019, 11, 323, 2182, 697, 1590, 4226, 2878, 1124, 79075, 46391, 151645, 198, 151644, 77091, 198, 151667, 271, 151668, 271], "input_tokens": 77, "method": "native_ar", "output_hash": "55814caaaf6be6c3824e05e79175da15bb79f05c8cd9af2ea0cb3de244a76c68", "output_ids": [1654, 525, 2661, 279, 13934, 315, 2326, 17228, 315, 264, 3070, 64761, 839, 2396, 95518, 2303, 14085, 198, 7, 20, 11, 220, 18, 701, 59, 320, 21, 11, 220, 23, 701, 59, 320, 22, 11, 220, 19, 340, 14085, 2303, 437, 279, 11737, 11936, 374, 4930, 87, 11, 379, 15087, 11, 448, 279, 2971, 429, 400, 87, 861, 220, 22, 12947, 1205, 525, 311, 1477, 279, 897, 315, 400, 87, 488, 379, 3, 382, 44364, 14374, 14822, 220, 16, 25, 70894, 279, 5888, 315, 264, 57030, 839, 2396, 271, 641, 264, 3070, 64761, 839, 2396, 97219, 279, 3070, 453, 13607, 11067, 525, 6144, 323, 15279, 97219, 323, 279, 3070, 8579, 6113, 1127, 14840, 439, 1817, 1008, 334, 382, 1986, 3363, 429, 279, 3070, 16106